/**
 * @defgroup motor_power_factor_calculator Motor Power Factor Calculator
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief Estimates the fraction of apparent electrical power used as real motor input power.
 * @details
 * How much of a motor's apparent electrical demand represents real electrical input? This calculation
 * estimates power factor at a supplied operating load. It helps pump and fan assessments relate current
 * to electrical input, while distinguishing motor efficiency from electrical power factor. Efficiency
 * describes shaft output relative to real input; power factor describes real input relative to apparent power.
 *
 * @heading{Final Result and Interpretation}
 * The returned result is a dimensionless fraction:
 * @formula{motor-pf-ratio; PF=\frac{P_{in}}{S}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{PF; Motor power factor as a fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{P_{in}; Real electrical input power; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{S; Apparent electrical power; \kilo\volt\ampere}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * A result of 0.78 means approximately 78 percent power factor, not 78 percent motor efficiency.
 * An ordinary consuming-motor interpretation expects a value between zero and one. The calculator does
 * not enforce that range: an inconsistent input combination can produce a larger, negative or nonfinite
 * result. Such a result calls for checking the inputs and model assumptions, not interpreting it as a
 * physically valid operating point. A single motor result does not establish plant-wide power factor.
 *
 * [DOE's motor sourcebook, printed page 15](https://www1.eere.energy.gov/manufacturing/tech_assistance/pdfs/motor.pdf#page=19)
 * explains the real-to-apparent power ratio and the relationship to lightly loaded motors.
 * Its [three-phase measurement example, printed page 42](https://www1.eere.energy.gov/manufacturing/tech_assistance/pdfs/motor.pdf#page=46)
 * uses voltage, current and power factor to obtain real power.
 * These references support the electrical relationships; they do not establish the provenance of this
 * calculator's near-zero threshold, fixed reference voltage or empirical loss ratio.
 *
 * @heading{Choosing the Calculation Path}
 * 1. Supply rated shaft power in hp, shaft load and operating efficiency as fractions, line current in A,
 *    and line-to-line voltage in V. Use quantities for a consistent operating point; the voltage input is
 *    named ratedVoltage but the loaded path uses its supplied value directly.
 * 2. Supply complete motor characteristics: frequency, speed, efficiency class and specified efficiency.
 *    These four fields are used only in the near-zero path, but remain required in the public input.
 * 3. If the absolute load fraction is strictly below 0.001, estimate real input from zero-load losses.
 *    This includes zero and small negative loads; exactly +0.001 and -0.001 take the loaded path.
 * 4. Otherwise infer real input from shaft load and supplied operating efficiency, then divide by
 *    apparent power. The two paths are retained separately and need not join continuously at the threshold.
 *
 * Enter 0.75 for 75 percent load and 0.90 for 90 percent efficiency. There is no automatic percentage
 * conversion. The threshold corresponds to 0.1 percent of rated shaft load; it is a retained model switch,
 * not a general physical definition of an unloaded motor.
 *
 * @heading{Loaded Motor: From Shaft Load to Electrical Demand}
 * The final loaded result is:
 * @formula{motor-pf-loaded;
 *   PF=\frac{L P_r c_{hp}}{I_L\eta_m V_{LL}\sqrt{3}/1000}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{PF; Calculated motor power factor; \unitless}
 * @symrow{L; Shaft load as a fraction of rated shaft power; \unitless}
 * @symrow{P_r; Rated shaft power; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{c_{hp}; Retained horsepower conversion of 0.746; \kilo\watt\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{I_L; RMS line current; \ampere}
 * @symrow{\eta_m; Operating motor efficiency as a fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{V_{LL}; Supplied RMS line-to-line voltage; \volt}
 * @symrow{\sqrt{3}; Balanced three-phase line-quantity factor; \unitless}
 * @symrow{1000; Volt-amperes per kilovolt-ampere; \volt\ampere\per\kilo\volt\ampere}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Shaft power alone omits electrical losses. Dividing it by efficiency estimates the real electrical
 * input required to deliver that shaft power:
 * @formula{motor-pf-shaft-input; P_{shaft}=L P_r c_{hp},\qquad P_{in}=\frac{P_{shaft}}{\eta_m}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_{shaft}; Motor shaft output at the supplied load; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{P_{in}; Estimated real electrical input; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{L; Fraction of rated shaft load; \unitless}
 * @symrow{P_r; Rated shaft power; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{c_{hp}; Retained conversion of 0.746; \kilo\watt\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{\eta_m; Supplied operating efficiency fraction; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * For balanced sinusoidal three-phase conditions, voltage and current give apparent power:
 * @formula{motor-pf-apparent; S=\frac{\sqrt{3} V_{LL} I_L}{1000}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{S; Apparent electrical input power; \kilo\volt\ampere}
 * @symrow{V_{LL}; RMS line-to-line voltage; \volt}
 * @symrow{I_L; RMS line current; \ampere}
 * @symrow{\sqrt{3}; Balanced three-phase factor; \unitless}
 * @symrow{1000; Volt-amperes per kilovolt-ampere; \volt\ampere\per\kilo\volt\ampere}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The loaded path uses the supplied efficiency rather than recalculating it from frequency, speed or
 * efficiency class. Those four model fields cannot correct an inappropriate operating efficiency.
 *
 * @heading{Near Zero Load: Electrical Loss Remains}
 * An idling energized motor can consume real power even though shaft output is zero. Dividing zero
 * shaft power by zero operating efficiency would not estimate that demand. Instead, the retained model
 * obtains zero-load electrical loss from @ref motor_efficiency_calculator, evaluated at quarter load.
 * Its modeled quarter-load efficiency supports the loss estimate:
 * @formula{motor-pf-quarter-loss;
 *   P_{loss,25}=\left(\frac{1}{\eta_{25}}-1\right)P_r c_{hp}(0.25)
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_{loss,25}; Modeled quarter-load electrical loss; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{\eta_{25}; Motor-efficiency model's quarter-load efficiency; \unitless}
 * @symrow{P_r; Rated motor shaft power; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{c_{hp}; Conversion of 0.746; \kilo\watt\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{0.25; Quarter-load fraction; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The inherited loss ratio estimates the real input for the entire near-zero branch:
 * @formula{motor-pf-zero-loss; P_{in,0}=P_{loss,0}=0.8P_{loss,25}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{P_{in,0}; Real input used in the near-zero branch; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{P_{loss,0}; Modeled zero-load loss; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{P_{loss,25}; Quarter-load electrical loss; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{0.8; Retained empirical zero-to-quarter-load loss ratio; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The final near-zero result uses a fixed voltage reference:
 * @formula{motor-pf-near-zero; PF_0=\frac{P_{loss,0}}{V_0\sqrt{3}I_L/1000},\qquad V_0=460\,\mathrm{V}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{PF_0; Power factor returned for abs(load fraction) below 0.001; \unitless}
 * @symrow{P_{loss,0}; Modeled real input at zero load; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{V_0; Retained fixed three-phase voltage reference of 460; \volt}
 * @symrow{I_L; Supplied line current, still used directly; \ampere}
 * @symrow{\sqrt{3}; Balanced three-phase factor; \unitless}
 * @symrow{1000; Volt-amperes per kilovolt-ampere; \volt\ampere\per\kilo\volt\ampere}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Supplied operating efficiency and voltage are ignored here. Changing ratedVoltage from 460 to 230
 * does not change this branch's result for the same motor characteristics and current. The fixed 460 V,
 * 0.8 loss ratio and 0.001 switch are preserved legacy assumptions with unverified original provenance.
 * The result is a model estimate at that reference, not a general measurement-based PF at any voltage.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Examples}
 * A 100 hp motor at load fraction 0.75 has shaft output 55.95 kW. With efficiency 0.90 its inferred real
 * input is 62.1666666667 kW. At 460 V and 100 A, apparent power is 79.6743371482 kVA. Therefore:
 * @formula{motor-pf-loaded-example;
 *   PF_{example}=\frac{(0.75)(100)(0.746)}{(100)(0.90)(460)\sqrt{3}/1000}=0.780259602927
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{PF_{example}; Loaded example result, approximately 78.026 percent; \unitless}
 * @symrow{0.75; Shaft load fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{100\text{ (numerator)}; Rated shaft power; \horsepower}
 * @symrow{0.746; Horsepower conversion; \kilo\watt\per\horsepower}
 * @symrow{100\text{ (denominator)}; Line current; \ampere}
 * @symrow{0.90; Operating efficiency fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{460; Supplied line-to-line voltage; \volt}
 * @symrow{\sqrt{3}; Three-phase factor; \unitless}
 * @symrow{1000; Volt-amperes per kilovolt-ampere; \volt\ampere\per\kilo\volt\ampere}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * For the near-zero example, use 60 Hz, 1780 rpm, ENERGY_EFFICIENT, specified efficiency 0 (ignored
 * for that class), rated power 100 hp, zero load and 30 A. The inherited efficiency model yields
 * quarter-load efficiency 0.921519665276. Quarter-load loss is approximately 1.58830928710 kW and
 * zero-load loss is 1.27064742968 kW. Apparent power at the fixed reference is 23.9023011445 kVA:
 * @formula{motor-pf-zero-example;
 *   PF_{0,example}=\frac{1.27064742968}{(460)\sqrt{3}(30)/1000}=0.053160046056
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{PF_{0,example}; Near-zero example result, approximately 5.316 percent; \unitless}
 * @symrow{1.27064742968; Modeled zero-load electrical loss; \kilo\watt}
 * @symrow{460; Fixed reference voltage; \volt}
 * @symrow{\sqrt{3}; Three-phase factor; \unitless}
 * @symrow{30; Supplied line current; \ampere}
 * @symrow{1000; Volt-amperes per kilovolt-ampere; \volt\ampere\per\kilo\volt\ampere}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Both examples are regression fixtures captured from the fully initialized legacy calculator.
 * Small nonzero loads below the threshold still use zero-load loss; no shaft-power term is added there.
 *
 * @heading{Assumptions, Boundaries and Inappropriate Uses}
 * - Use consistent three-phase RMS line quantities and operating load/efficiency. This model does not
 *   characterize unbalanced phases, VFD waveform distortion, single-phase machines, or transient starting.
 * - For ordinary loaded operation, rated power, current, voltage and efficiency should be positive;
 *   efficiency is a fraction normally at most one. The function neither checks these conditions nor caps load.
 * - Near-zero modeling inherits the motor-efficiency calculator's requirements and data limits. PREMIUM
 *   uses 2-, 4-, and 6-pole curves; higher classified pole counts use the six-pole curve rather than a
 *   dedicated higher-pole model. SPECIFIED needs positive efficiency for a meaningful estimate;
 *   negative specified efficiency propagates the model's error. Other classes ignore specified efficiency.
 * - Zero current, loaded zero voltage or loaded zero efficiency can produce infinity or NaN. Negative
 *   inputs retain signed arithmetic; a PF above one is not clamped. There is no fallback or sentinel result.
 * - This estimates a ratio, not capacitor sizing, plant utility penalties, energy use, or motor efficiency.
 *   A reference-voltage near-zero result is not sufficient for evaluating actual PF at a different voltage.
 *
 * @heading{Calculation API and Migration}
 * C++ callers include motorDriven/motor/motor_power_factor.h:
 * @code{.cpp}
 * const motor_power_factor::Input input = {Motor::LineFrequency::FREQ60, 1780,
 *     Motor::EfficiencyClass::ENERGY_EFFICIENT, 0, 100, 0.75, 100, 0.90, 460};
 * const double power_factor = motor_power_factor::calculate(input);
 * @endcode
 * TypeScript callers use a complete plain object:
 * @code{.ts}
 * import createModule, { type MotorPowerFactorInput } from 'measur-tools-suite';
 * const module = await createModule();
 * const input: MotorPowerFactorInput = {
 *     lineFrequency: module.LineFrequency.FREQ60, motorRpm: 1780,
 *     efficiencyClass: module.MotorEfficiencyClass.ENERGY_EFFICIENT, specifiedEfficiency: 0,
 *     motorRatedPower: 100, loadFactor: 0.75, motorCurrent: 100, motorEfficiency: 0.90, ratedVoltage: 460
 * };
 * const powerFactor: number = module.calculateMotorPowerFactor(input);
 * @endcode
 * Replace legacy MotorPowerFactor constructors and calculate() calls with this function. Supply all nine
 * fields even when the loaded branch ignores model data. Remove delete() calls; no runtime object is allocated.
 * The old class, accessors, header and runtime constructor are removed without compatibility wrappers.
 * MotorPerformance now supplies complete nameplate data for its formerly uninitialized near-zero path;
 * its existing loaded results and percentage output convention are preserved.
 */
